---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";

// Every number on this page comes from
// `.uses/parley-demo/benchmarks/2026-09-26-text-system-swap.md`, whose two
// columns are two implementations of the one trait below. The trade is stated
// as it was measured: parley wins wrapping and metrics, and loses rasterization
// and a one-off startup cost.
const comparison = [
  {
    op: "new — cold, registers one face",
    vanilla: "5.75 µs",
    parley: "44.24 ms",
    note: "7,700× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
  {
    op: "resolve_font",
    vanilla: "67.9 ns",
    parley: "99.5 ns",
    note: "1.5× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
  {
    op: "bounding_box",
    vanilla: "18.1 ns",
    parley: "4.8 ns",
    note: "3.8× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "line_metrics (ascent + descent + baseline)",
    vanilla: "68.0 ns",
    parley: "5.9 ns",
    note: "11.5× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "layout_line (a 3-line paragraph)",
    vanilla: "131.30 µs",
    parley: "83.31 µs",
    note: "1.58× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "wrap_line (same paragraph, 420 px)",
    vanilla: "79.07 µs",
    parley: "33.27 µs",
    note: "2.4× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "raster_bounds, cached",
    vanilla: "22.3 ns",
    parley: "62.5 ns",
    note: "2.8× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
  {
    op: "raster_bounds, uncached",
    vanilla: "3.36 µs",
    parley: "7.79 µs",
    note: "2.3× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
  {
    op: "rasterize_glyph",
    vanilla: "3.06 µs",
    parley: "15.48 µs",
    note: "5.1× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
];

const cache = [
  { impl: "vanilla", uncached: "3.36 µs", cached: "22.3 ns", buys: "151×" },
  {
    impl: "parley 1.21.1",
    uncached: "7.79 µs",
    cached: "62.5 ns",
    buys: "125×",
  },
  {
    impl: "parley 1.21.0 (previous)",
    uncached: "36.30 µs",
    cached: "61.2 ns",
    buys: "593×",
  },
];

const delta = [
  {
    op: "rasterize_glyph",
    from: "78.30 µs",
    to: "15.48 µs",
    note: "5.1× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "raster_bounds, uncached",
    from: "36.30 µs",
    to: "7.79 µs",
    note: "4.7× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "layout_line",
    from: "95.93 µs",
    to: "83.31 µs",
    note: "1.15× faster",
    dir: "win",
  },
  {
    op: "wrap_line",
    from: "15.56 µs",
    to: "33.27 µs",
    note: "2.1× slower",
    dir: "loss",
  },
  {
    op: "new",
    from: "33.88 µs",
    to: "44.24 ms",
    note: "host font enumeration",
    dir: "loss",
  },
];

const install = `<span class="c"># Cargo.toml</span>
[dependencies]
bite-gpui = <span class="s">"1.21"</span>
bite-gp-parley = <span class="s">"1.21"</span>`;

const swap = `<span class="k">use</span> gpui::<span class="m">*</span>;
<span class="k">use</span> gpui_parley::<span class="t">ParleyTextSystem</span>;

<span class="k">fn</span> <span class="f">main</span>() {
    <span class="f">application</span>()
        <span class="c">// ParleyTextSystem::new() already returns an Arc<Self></span>
        .<span class="f">with_text_system</span>(<span class="t">ParleyTextSystem</span>::<span class="f">new</span>())
        .<span class="f">run</span>(|<span class="m">cx</span>: <span class="m">&amp;mut</span> <span class="t">App</span>| {
            cx.<span class="f">open_window</span>(<span class="t">WindowOptions</span>::<span class="f">default</span>(), |<span class="m">_</span>, <span class="m">cx</span>| {
                cx.<span class="f">new</span>(|_| <span class="t">MyView</span>)
            })
            .<span class="f">unwrap</span>();
        });
}`;

const verify = `<span class="c"># the harness the table above came from, in the usage suite</span>
cd parley-demo
<span class="t">CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG</span>=0 <span class="t">CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG</span>=0 \\
<span class="t">CARGO_PROFILE_BENCH_DEBUG</span>=0 cargo bench --bench text_system`;
---

<Page
  title="Using gpui_parley — bite-gpui"
  description="Swap GPUI's text engine for Parley through one trait, and the measured trade that comes with it."
  kicker="Swap · TextSystem"
  heading="Bring your own"
  accent="text engine."
  lede="The engine draws text through a single trait. bite-gp-parley is a second implementation of it — install the crate, hand it to the application at startup, and every existing div() keeps rendering. Nothing else in the tree changes."
  current="parley"
>
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The seam is one trait.</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">TextSystem</span> lives in
        <span class="mono">gpui_engine</span> and covers shaping, layout and
        rasterization. The facade exposes exactly one way to replace it:
        <span class="mono">Application::with_text_system</span>. A text engine
        written outside the repository plugs in through that method, which is
        what makes this an install rather than a patch.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-block">
      <pre><code set:html={install}></code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="code-block">
      <pre><code set:html={swap}></code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">No rename needed</span>
      <p>
        Cargo exposes a dependency under its <em>library</em> name, and the
        published package keeps its upstream lib target — so
        <code>bite-gp-parley</code> is <code>use gpui_parley::…</code>, with no
        <code>package =</code> key in your manifest.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What it changes, measured.</h2>
      <p>
        Both columns below are the same operations on the same font and the same
        16 px text, called through <span class="mono">&amp;dyn TextSystem</span>
        so the implementation is the only variable. The
        <span class="mono">vanilla</span> column is what a Linux user gets
        without asking for anything —<span class="mono">DefaultTextSystem</span>{" "}
        over
        <span class="mono">CosmicTextSystem</span>. The
        <span class="mono">parley</span> column is
        <span class="mono">bite-gp-parley 1.21.1</span>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>operation</th>
            <th class="num">vanilla</th>
            <th class="num">parley</th>
            <th>result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {comparison.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class="mono">{row.op}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{row.vanilla}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.parley}</td>
              <td class={row.dir}>{row.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="table-note">
      Mean estimates from criterion, 100 measurements per benchmark. Host: Intel
      i7-8750H, linux x86_64 — a laptop, not a controlled benchmarking host.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>It is a trade, not a win.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Parley is faster where the engine drives text in bulk: wrapping the same
        paragraph is <strong>2.4×</strong> faster, a three-line layout
        <strong>1.58×</strong>, and the per-font metrics — the numbers a
        rearchitected engine reads constantly — <strong>3.8×</strong> and
        <strong>11.5×</strong>. It is slower at putting one glyph on screen:
        <strong>15.48 µs</strong> to rasterize a 16 px glyph against vanilla's
        <strong>3.06 µs</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Neither number is a defect on its own. A project that swaps the text
        system is buying shaping — complex-script support, font fallback, family
        stacks — and paying for it in rasterization. The point of publishing
        both columns is that this is now visible before you install it rather
        than after.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="callout-title">Startup costs 44 ms</span>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">ParleyTextSystem::new</span> is 7,700× vanilla's, and
        that is the host's fonts, not a regression in the same work: it builds a
        fontique collection with
        <span class="mono">system_fonts: true</span>, enumerating every font on
        the machine. The previous release, which did not, constructed in
        <strong>33.88 µs</strong>. It is paid once per process, and it is what
        buys the host-font fallback the crate exists for — worth knowing before
        installing it in something that starts often.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The cache both systems share.</h2>
      <p>
        Memoizing glyph raster bounds is the mechanism behind the dramatic ratio
        on the marketing page, so the honest measurement is cached against
        uncached <em>within</em> one implementation:
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>implementation</th>
            <th class="num">uncached</th>
            <th class="num">cached</th>
            <th>the cache buys</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {cache.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class="mono">{row.impl}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{row.uncached}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.cached}</td>
              <td class="win">{row.buys}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="table-note">
      The two parley rows differ only in the uncached path: 36.30 µs down to
      7.79 µs, with the cached path unchanged at roughly 62 ns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What the 1.21.1 release changed.</h2>
      <p>
        The rasterization cost above was not always 15.48 µs. The hinting
        instance is now cached per face and size instead of rebuilt once per
        rasterized glyph, which is the whole of the improvement below — measured
        on the same host within an hour of each other.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>operation</th>
            <th class="num">1.21.0</th>
            <th class="num">1.21.1</th>
            <th>result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {delta.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class="mono">{row.op}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{row.from}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.to}</td>
              <td class={row.dir}>{row.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="table-note">
      The <span class="mono">wrap_line</span> loss and the metric costs in the
      first table are the same host-font-database change as the 44 ms: each
      lookup now consults the shaper's own database rather than a table held by
      the crate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Reproduce it.</h2>
      <p>
        The harness is one file, and its two columns are two implementations
        behind the same trait — one implementation measured alone would say what
        Parley costs, not what <em>choosing</em> Parley costs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-block">
      <pre><code set:html={verify}></code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        That harness is part of the project's own usage suite, which is not
        published yet — so the table above is backed by a recorded run, with its
        command, toolchain, host and every sample, rather than by a repository
        you can clone today. What you <em>can</em> run right now is the crate's
        cost examples, which is where the frame-budget figures on the one-pager
        come from:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/bite-gpui/gpui_parley"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            bite-gpui/gpui_parley
          </a>
          — the crate, its tests and its <span class="mono">raster_cost</span>,
          <span class="mono">vector_cost</span> and
          <span class="mono">frame_cost</span> examples.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://crates.io/crates/bite-gp-parley"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            bite-gp-parley on crates.io
          </a>
          — the published artefact every number here was taken against.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
